+# Phase 1 Contract: `accounts` CLI — password-reset delta
+
+**Feature**: `002-password-reset` | **Date**: 2026-11-16
+
+A **delta** over [001's CLI contract](../../001-local-account-manager/contracts/cli-contract.md),
+which stays authoritative for the five existing commands. This file states only what changes:
+two new commands, one new option, four new diagnostics. Error numbering continues from E13, so an
+identifier like `E15` is unambiguous repository-wide.
+
+Everything in 001's contract that is not restated here is unchanged, and FR-060 makes that a
+requirement rather than an expectation: the five existing commands keep their invocation form,
+stdin protocol, output text, exit statuses, and store effects exactly.
+
+The in-process seam is unchanged: `accounts.cli.main(argv, stdin, stdout, stderr) -> int`.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m accounts --store PATH reset-token USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]
+python -m accounts --store PATH reset USERNAME
+```
+
+| Element | Form | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--store PATH` | Unchanged — required, one value, accepted anywhere on the line | `python -m accounts reset alice --store ./s.json` is equally valid, as it is for every existing command (R-13) |
+| `COMMAND` | The choice set grows to `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete`, `reset-token`, `reset` | Exact, lower-case. `reset-token` is spelled with a hyphen |
+| `USERNAME` | One positional value | Passed through as given; format is still validated only by `register` |
+| `--ttl SECONDS` | Optional, `reset-token` only | A string of ASCII decimal digits, value `>= 1`. Default 900. See E16/E17 |
+
+**No token and no password is ever a command-line argument** (FR-003, FR-048). The token is read
+from stdin, never from `argv`, so it never reaches a process listing or a shell history.
+
+### `--ttl` and `--help`
+
+`--ttl` is registered with `help=argparse.SUPPRESS`, so it appears in neither the usage line nor
+the options list of `python -m accounts --help`. This is deliberate and required: an existing
+FR-003 test asserts that the `--`-prefixed tokens in `--help` output are exactly
+`{--store, --help}`, and FR-060/SC-011 require that test to pass unmodified. The option is fully
+functional and specified here; research.md R-13 records the decision and the alternative the
+project owner may prefer.
+
+Consequently, the only change to any usage message is the command list growing by two names:
+
+```text
+usage: python -m accounts [-h] --store PATH
+                          {register,login,unlock,passwd,delete,reset-token,reset}
+                          username
+```
+
+## Standard-input protocol
+
+Added to 001's table; the five existing rows are unchanged.
+
+| Command | Lines read | Order |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | 0 | — |
+| `reset` | 2 | the token, then the new password |
+
+Line handling is identical to passwords (FR-017, 001 R-06): one `readline` per value, exactly one
+trailing `"\n"` removed if present, every other character preserved. Length rules count
+characters, not bytes.
+
+Two distinctions this contract commits to:
+
+- **End of input is never an empty value.** Reaching EOF where the token line or the new-password
+  line was required is E14 or E9 — status 2 — not an empty token and not an empty password
+  (FR-055).
+- **An empty line is a value.** A present-but-empty token line is a token like any other; it
+  matches nothing, so it is E15 `invalid token` with status 1 (spec edge case).
+
+For `reset`, the account-existence check happens **before any line is read**, so a command naming
+a missing account exits 3 without consuming input — the same rule `unlock`, `passwd`, and
+`delete` already follow (FR-054, FR-034).
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Unchanged from 001. The two new commands use only statuses already defined, and **never use 4**
+(FR-061):
+
+| Status | Meaning for the new commands |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | Success |
+| 1 | Token rejected — wrong, superseded, already used, expired, or none outstanding |
+| 2 | Invalid input, usage error, or unusable store file |
+| 3 | Account does not exist |
+| 4 | *Never returned by `reset-token` or `reset`* |
+
+## Stream discipline
+
+Unchanged (FR-062): results on stdout, diagnostics on stderr, never the reverse, every message
+one line terminated by `"\n"`. On every success path stderr is empty; on every failure path
+stdout is empty. No token, password, salt, or verifier appears in any message (FR-058, FR-037).
+
+---
+
+## Success responses
+
+| Command | Status | stdout | stderr | Effect on the store |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | 0 | `TOKEN\n` — one line, the token and nothing else | *(empty)* | `reset_token` set to a fresh `{salt, verifier, expires_at}`, superseding any previous one. Password, `failed_logins`, and `locked` untouched. Durable on exit |
+| `reset` | 0 | `password reset\n` | *(empty)* | Password `salt`/`verifier` replaced; `reset_token` removed. `failed_logins` and `locked` untouched. Durable on exit |
+
+`password reset` is fixed verbatim by the feature description. The token line is the whole point
+of `reset-token`, and is the **only** place in the tool where a token is ever emitted (FR-058).
+
+`TOKEN` is 43 characters matching `\A[A-Za-z0-9_-]{43}\Z` (research.md R-09). Callers may rely on
+the line containing nothing else — no label, no prefix, no trailing space — so
+`TOKEN=$(... reset-token alice)` is safe.
+
+Both commands succeed on a **locked** account, with the same output and status as on an unlocked
+one, and a successful `reset` does **not** clear the lock (FR-047, FR-056).
+
+## Failure responses
+
+One message is fixed verbatim by the spec and MUST match exactly:
+
+- `invalid token` — FR-052
+
+The rest are this contract's choice, as in 001. Three reuse 001's existing text verbatim, which
+is deliberate: the condition is the same one, so the diagnostic should be too.
+
+| # | Condition | Command(s) | Status | stderr |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| E1 *(reused)* | New password shorter than 12 characters | `reset` | 2 | `password must be at least 12 characters` |
+| E4 *(reused)* | Account does not exist | `reset-token`, `reset` | 3 | `no such account: USERNAME` |
+| E9 *(reused)* | End of input after the token line | `reset` | 2 | `no new password on standard input` |
+| E14 | End of input where the token was required | `reset` | 2 | `no token on standard input` |
+| E15 | Token wrong, superseded, already used, expired, or none outstanding | `reset` | 1 | `invalid token` |
+| E16 | `--ttl` supplied with any command other than `reset-token` | `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete`, `reset` | 2 | argparse usage message, then `python -m accounts: error: --ttl is only valid with the reset-token command` |
+| E17 | `--ttl` is not a string of decimal digits, or is `0` | `reset-token` (and any command, since parsing precedes dispatch) | 2 | argparse usage message, then its `argument --ttl:` error line |
+| E10–E12 *(reused)* | Store unreadable / not a store / unwritable | both new commands | 2 | As 001 |
+
+E16 and E17 go through `argparse` (`parser.error` and a `type` callable respectively), so they
+have exactly the shape of 001's E13 — usage message, error line, status 2 — and an operator
+cannot tell a scoping error from any other usage error by its form.
+
+**E15 is uninformative on purpose.** All five conditions behind it produce byte-identical output
+and the same status, so the response never reveals which applied — in particular never that a
+guessed token was once real (FR-052, SC-013).
+
+**E17 covers the coercion cases.** `--ttl 1_000`, `--ttl +5`, `--ttl " 10 "`, `--ttl ١٢٣`,
+`--ttl 10.0`, `--ttl 1e3`, `--ttl -5`, `--ttl 0`, and `--ttl abc` are all status 2. `int()` would
+have silently accepted the first four as 1000, 5, 10, and 123 (research.md R-14).
+
+---
+
+## Check order per command
+
+The order is observable: it determines which of two simultaneous problems is reported. Derived in
+[research.md](../research.md) R-15.
+
+| Command | Order |
+|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | `--ttl` value (E17) → `--ttl` scope (E16) → store loads (E10/E11) → exists? (E4) → issue, supersede, save (E12 if the write fails) |
+| `reset` | store loads (E10/E11) → exists? (E4) → token line present (E14) → new-password line present (E9) → token valid? (E15) → new password length (E1) → replace, clear token, save (E12 if the write fails) |
+
+Five consequences the contract commits to:
+
+- **Wrong token *and* a too-short password → 1 (E15), not 2.** The length check is reachable only
+  after the token is accepted, so a status-2 reply can never confirm a token was real (FR-053) —
+  the same reasoning as `passwd`'s verify-before-length (FR-028).
+- **Wrong token *and* a missing password line → 2 (E9), not 1.** Both end-of-input checks precede
+  judging the token, so the missing line is reported first (FR-055, spec edge case).
+- **Unknown account *and* empty stdin → 3 (E4), and nothing is read.** Existence precedes every
+  read (FR-054).
+- **A too-short password leaves the token outstanding.** The immediately following attempt with
+  the same token and a 12-character password succeeds (FR-053, SC-015). This is the only `reset`
+  failure after which the token still works.
+- **A bad `--ttl` issues no token and leaves the store untouched**, because it fails in the parser
+  before the store is even read (FR-042).
+
+## Store side effects
+
+Added to 001's table; the existing rows are unchanged.
+
+| Outcome | Store written? |
+|---|---|
+| `reset-token` success | Yes |
+| `reset` success | Yes |
+| `reset` rejected — E15 `invalid token` (including an **expired** token) | **No.** The expired `reset_token` record is not swept; sweeping would be a write on a failure path and would let an observer distinguish "expired" from "wrong" by the file's mtime (research.md R-16) |
+| `reset` rejected — E1, E4, E9, E14 | No |
+| `reset-token` rejected — E4, E16, E17 | No |
+
+A rejected token is **not** a failed login: `failed_logins` is never incremented and no number of
+wrong tokens can lock an account (FR-056).
+
+## Store-format compatibility
+
+The store format stamp stays `version: 1`, and an account record gains one **optional** key,
+`reset_token`. A store file written before this feature therefore loads without complaint and
+every account in it reads as having no outstanding token (FR-059). Strict validation is
+preserved, not relaxed: an unknown record key, a misspelled `reset_tokens`, a malformed
+`reset_token`, or a non-finite `expires_at` is still a loud `StoreError` with status 2, never a
+defaulted or ignored value (FR-007). Details in [data-model.md](../data-model.md).
+
+---
+
+## Worked examples
+
+```console
+$ printf 'correct horse battery\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json register alice
+registered alice
+$ echo $?
+0
+
+# 1. The administrator issues a token. One line on stdout, nothing on stderr.
+$ PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json reset-token alice
+vkSJ26_QDBV-4OxJ7FvYTCx7OFNFKij_2t0kwHx-xKM
+$ echo $?
+0
+
+# 2. The user redeems it: token first, then the new password.
+$ printf 'vkSJ26_QDBV-4OxJ7FvYTCx7OFNFKij_2t0kwHx-xKM\na brand new secret\n' \
+    | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json reset alice
+password reset
+$ echo $?
+0
+
+# 3. The same token a second time. Already used, so it is rejected.
+$ printf 'vkSJ26_QDBV-4OxJ7FvYTCx7OFNFKij_2t0kwHx-xKM\nanother new secret\n' \
+    | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json reset alice
+invalid token                              # on stderr; stdout empty; password unchanged
+$ echo $?
+1
+
+# 4. A fresh token, but the new password is too short. The token survives.
+$ PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json reset-token alice --ttl 60
+TJj0lP8uXg-Qw2nR5sVbK1aYhZcD3eF7mN9oP0qRsTu
+$ printf 'TJj0lP8uXg-Qw2nR5sVbK1aYhZcD3eF7mN9oP0qRsTu\nshort\n' \
+    | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json reset alice
+password must be at least 12 characters    # on stderr; token still outstanding
+$ echo $?
+2
+$ printf 'TJj0lP8uXg-Qw2nR5sVbK1aYhZcD3eF7mN9oP0qRsTu\nlong enough secret\n' \
+    | PYTHONPATH=src python -m accounts --store ./store.json reset alice
+password reset
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+Token values above are illustrative; every run produces a different one (FR-040).
+
+> **Note on SC-010.** SC-010 requires "all four worked examples in the feature description" to
+> reproduce exactly, but the feature description recorded in `spec.md`'s **Input** contains no
+> worked examples — the same gap 001's contract records for SC-002. The four numbered above are
+> taken from the only fully specified stdout/stderr/status triples in the spec: User Story 1
+> scenarios 1 and 2, User Story 2 scenario 2, and User Story 3 scenarios 1 and 2.
+> `tests/test_reset_end_to_end.py` asserts these four. If the original description's examples
+> differ, that test file is the single place to correct.
